    "evidence_text": "Only in vitro studies that discuss the effect of statins on head and neck carcinoma were selected.   \n\n\nHMG-CoA reductase inhibitors and the risk of hip fractures in elderly patients.\n\nOBJECTIVE To determine whether the use of statins is associated with reduced hip fracture risk.   \n\n\nDESIGN Case-control study.   \n\n\nNo significant relationship was observed between use of nonstatin lipid-lowering agents and hip fracture risk.\n\nCONCLUSIONS These findings support an association between statin use by elderly patients and reduction in the risk of hip fracture.\n\nJAMA.\n\nMean follow-up was 4.1 years.\n\nNo evidence of an increased risk of cancer was observed.\n\nStatins in the primary prevention of cardiovascular disease\n\nSecondly, long-term statin use might cause adverse effects, such as incident diabetes mellitus.\n\nPreoperative statin treatment reduces systemic inflammatory response and myocardial damage in cardiac surgery.\n\nPleiotropic effects of statins.\n\nStatins are potent inhibitors of cholesterol biosynthesis.\n\nStatin use and risk of gallstone disease followed by cholecystectomy.\n\nData on this association in humans are scarce.   \n\n\nSimvastatin reduces platelet-endocardium adhesion in atrial fibrillation.",
